diff --git a/guidance.md b/guidance.md
index a6994b1..0b59c18 100644
--- a/guidance.md
+++ b/guidance.md
@@ -1 +1,15 @@
 Use the provided spreadsheet tools to complete the requested workbook. Preserve source inputs.
+
+1. Inspect first. Run `workbook_inspect` on the source and read every label, unit and comment. Note which cells are intentionally blank so they stay blank, and identify which rows are inputs and which are outputs.
+
+2. Derive each formula from the row and column labels, units and comments - not from a number you expect to see. Formulas must use A1 references to the workbook's own input cells so the model stays live; a hard-coded result is not an acceptable answer. When a metric builds on another computed row, reference that row rather than repeating its arithmetic.
+
+3. Write with `workbook_write`, `recalculate: true`, and a `checks` array for any model with arithmetic relationships (ratios, totals, roll-forwards, schedules). A check is an independent statement about the recalculated values that must hold for the model to be internally consistent, for example a margin equal to profit over revenue, a closing balance equal to opening minus repayments, or a total equal to the sum of its parts. Checks that fail are reported as warnings - fix the formula, do not relax the check.
+
+4. Verify before you stop:
+- `recalculation.recalculated` must be true; if it is false, fix the cause instead of delivering an unchecked file.
+- every formula you wrote has a numeric value in `values`, with no engine error such as `#VALUE!`, `#REF!` or `#NAME?`;
+- no `warnings` remain; a cell whose value was stored as text must be rewritten as a formula;
+- the numbers agree with the plan from the labels (units, signs, ratio ranges).
+
+5. Only write into destination cells the request asks for. Never overwrite source inputs, labels, year headers, units or intentional blanks, and keep the original comments.
